WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.440
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar and you're watching Mind Your Decisions.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.920
Here's a fun puzzle I received by email.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.140
The number abcdef is a special six-digit number.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:17.069
Imagine placing it as the first row of a six-by-six table.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:23.699
We find the rest of the rows by multiplying the number by two, three, four, five, and six.

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:27.119
This number has a special property.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:35.700
In every row there are six different digits with no repeats and in every column there are six different digits with no repeats.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.800
In other words, this is a Latin square.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.020
And I should mention you're multiplying the entire number in each row.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:47.680
For example, if the six digit number is 123456,

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.860
Then Row 2 would equal 246912.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:59.020
You're multiplying the entire number by two, three, four, five, and six - not each individual digit in each column.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.460
So, the puzzle is: Can you figure out a value for abcdef?

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:11.020
Give this problem a try, and when you're ready keep watching the video for a solution.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:30.920
So, I solved the seemingly impossible problem nearly instantly because I knew the fractions of seven are cyclical.

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:37.979
We start out with 1/7, which equals 14, 28, and 57, repeating.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:46.600
You can remember this as 14-28-57. We can find the rest of the fractions by 7 as rotations of this number.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:50.260
For example, to multiply this number by 2,

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:57.700
we start out with 142857 repeating and then we shift the digits to the next smallest number.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:03.260
So, to multiply by 2 we find the second smallest digit in these six numbers.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:09.900
This would be the number 2. So we shift over the numbers so we start out with the number 2

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:15.320
So we multiply by 2 to get 28, 57, and 14.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:21.580
We can do this to find the rest of the fractions. To multiply by 3, we start out with 142857,

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:23.910
we then look for the third smallest digit,

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:29.429
which is the number 4. We start out with the number 4 and shift over all of the other digits.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:34.800
We continue this process to figure out all of the other fractions of 7.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:40.950
You'll notice these are all rotations of the same six numbers.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.740
And we end up with all of the fractions of 7.

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:47.860
So, how does this help us solve the puzzle?

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:52.320
Well, what we'll do is we'll go from fractions to 6-digit numbers.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:58.960
So we'll just remove the fraction parts and we now end up with the number 142857,

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.220
and all of these multiplications are still valid.

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:07.540
We'll now arrange these numbers into a six-by-six table, and there you go!

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:14.250
In every row, there are six different digits with no repeats, and in every column there are six different digits with no repeats.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:16.680
This is a Latin square.

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:25.020
ABCDEF is equal to 142857 and I'm pretty sure this is the only solution to this puzzle because this is the only

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:29.789
six-digit cyclical number in decimals, which doesn't have a leading zero

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:36.570
But I leave it open to you if you can find any other possibilities or prove why this is the only possibility

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:39.780
Did you figure it out?

